{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade in maintainability because the commit trajectory is non-ergodic: each locally correct commit biases the probability of future commits in ways that no individual developer, who only observes a small temporal slice, can fully sample. The codebase's state space thus diverges from any developer's ergodic mental model, which is implicitly built on the assumption that sampling a subset of the system's history yields reliable expectations for the whole. This divergence makes future changes incrementally harder because developers are perpetually optimizing against a stale, non-representative prior of the system's actual structural constraints."}
